Post-Viral Reactive Respiratory Thoracic Syndromes
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Persistent post-viral dry hacking cough, Cough exacerbated by cold air/irritants, Vague mild chest tightness, Normal chest radiograph

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-040
Reason for Visit: Unremitting paroxysms of a severe dry cough causing gagging, retching, and significant chest tightness, requiring urgent assessment for secondary pneumonia or acute bronchospasm.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing unrelenting coughing fits causing extreme shortness of breath, retching, and severe chest discomfort following a viral illness. Urgent physical assessment is required to auscultate the lungs, rule out secondary bacterial consolidation, and provide immediate pharmacological relief for severe airway hyperreactivity prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My chest is tight and I can't stop this dry, barking cough; it's making me gag and I can't catch my breath."
History of Present Illness: The patient recovered from an acute respiratory viral illness (fever, purulent sputum) 3 weeks ago. Since then, the patient describes a chronic, persistent, and entirely non-productive dry hacking cough. The cough is severely exacerbated by specific irritants such as cold air and prolonged talking. The patient notes a sensation of a distinct 'tickle' and severe hyper-reactive localized irritation within the upper airways. Over the last 48 hours, the patient has developed vague, diffuse, and mild chest tightness unassociated with severe coronary ischaemia, alongside mild to moderate exertional breathlessness entirely out of proportion to their normal fitness. The frequent severe paroxysms of coughing result in gagging and intense retching. Highly refractory to short courses of standard empiric over-the-counter cough suppressants.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but breathless and exhausted from constant coughing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation due to night-time coughing paroxysms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Fatigued, holding chest during coughing fits, taking sips of water to suppress the tickle.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals normal baseline oxygen saturation. Chest auscultation reveals clear, equal air entry bilaterally with an absolute absence of persistent fine inspiratory crackles or dense consolidation. Mild, transient expiratory wheeze is noted on forced expiration, indicative of bronchial hyperreactivity. Cardiovascular exam is completely normal. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Persistent post-viral dry hacking cough, Cough exacerbated by cold air/irritants, Vague mild chest tightness, and Normal chest radiograph (clinically clear chest; imaging to be done if persists).
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty bottles of over-the-counter cough syrup on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular inhalers or asthma medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Reactive Airway Disease (Post-Infectious Cough) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Secondary Bacterial Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute absence of high fever, purulent/rust-colored sputum, and focal lobar crackles or dullness on chest auscultation.

Pertussis (Whooping Cough) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of the classic inspiratory "whoop" or post-tussive vomiting, though the prolonged duration of a paroxysmal cough makes it a differential requiring a PCR swab if it persists.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chest tightness is vague, mild, clearly linked to coughing paroxysms, and entirely non-exertional (cardiac).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a severe, debilitating post-infectious hyper-reactive airway syndrome, causing unremitting coughing, retching, and severe chest tightness. The presence of chest discomfort and breathlessness necessitated an urgent, direct clinical examination to confidently rule out a silent secondary bacterial pneumonia, assess for acute severe bronchospasm, and immediately prescribe targeted inhaled corticosteroids/bronchodilators to break the severe paroxysmal cycle. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a risk of undetected respiratory decompensation or severe muscular chest injury from coughing, thus fulfilling the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe a combination inhaled corticosteroid/long-acting beta-agonist (ICS/LABA) or a short course of oral corticosteroids (e.g., Prednisolone 25mg for 3-5 days) to rapidly forcefully suppress the severe bronchial inflammation. Provide a short-acting bronchodilator (Salbutamol) via a spacer for immediate acute relief of the chest tightness.
Positive Behavioral Change: By utilizing a dedicated spacer device strictly every single time the inhaler is used, the patient will ensure the medication reaches the deep inflamed airways rather than hitting the back of the throat; experiencing immediate relief of the chest tightness will powerfully reinforce the slightly cumbersome habit of using the spacer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Asthma spacer device.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to reassess lung fields and consider pulmonary function testing if the cough persists.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops sudden, sharp, stabbing chest pain (concerning for pneumothorax from coughing), begins coughing up large amounts of fresh blood, or develops severe shortness of breath at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; a formal chest X-ray and pulmonary function tests may be required if symptoms do not respond to therapy.
Clinical Course: The initial infection stripped the protective lining of your airways, leaving the raw nerve endings exposed. This hyper-reactivity can take 3 to 8 weeks to completely heal and resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The violent coughing fits can occasionally cause small tears in the lung tissue. Seek immediate emergency care if you suddenly develop a sharp, stabbing pain in one side of your chest that makes it impossible to breathe deeply.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus is gone, but it left your airways severely inflamed, twitchy, and hypersensitive to cold air. Over-the-counter cough syrups will not work because the problem is deep lung inflammation. The steroid inhaler will coat the airways and aggressively shut down this inflammatory response.
Actionable Steps: Stop all over-the-counter cough syrups. Always use the spacer with your inhaler. Avoid sudden changes in air temperature and strong perfumes.

